{
  "id": "T18_60_a_requirement_keeps_its_own_interpretation_under_a_project_normalization",
  "family": "T18",
  "rules": [
    "7.6.13",
    "7.6.14",
    "7.3.8",
    "5.5.3",
    "9.3.1",
    "9.3.2",
    "9.3.3",
    "9.5.7",
    "12.20"
  ],
  "description": "Three expectations require A-602 to show a fire rating of \"90 min\", and A-602's Monday issue shows \"1.5 h\". Door 105's context names the fixture fire-rating interpretation, Door 115's the fixture set interpretation of fire_rating, and Door 102's none. Door 105 is fulfilled on Monday under its own interpretation. A Wednesday normalization of the project names the fire-rating interpretation, and a Friday one names literal comparison. Only Door 102's property, whose context names none, is fulfilled by the Wednesday normalization, and that write records nothing for the other two. At every cutoff a frame naming no interpretation reads each requirement under its own interpretation from its recorded acts: Door 105 is fulfilled from Monday and never listed by open work or what remains; Door 115 stays outstanding, although the project's interpretation would equate its values; Door 102 is outstanding until Wednesday and fulfilled from then. A frame naming the fire-rating interpretation computes all three under it and lists nothing.",
  "note": "7.6.13: \"An expectation's interpretation is the one its context names, at every instant, and no normalization changes it.\" 7.3.8 *Interpretation in force*: \"A normalization governs only what names no interpretation of its own.\" 7.6.14: a read \"evaluates an expectation under its own when its frame names no interpretation, whether the expectation's context names one or not, or when its frame names the interpretation that the expectation's context names. A frame that names another interpretation, or requests literal comparison (4.6.1), evaluates the expectation under that one at every instant.\" 9.5.7: \"A frame that names no interpretation reads every unit under its own interpretation, so it never lists as outstanding a property that is fulfilled under its own (7.6.13).\" A frame naming none therefore does not compute Door 105 and Door 115 under the interpretation in force, under which Door 105 would be outstanding until Wednesday and Door 115 fulfilled from Wednesday.",
